### SR-S4 — no overdelivery allowance; wrong month basis

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Source attack** | STD-04 (`53 §2`), STD-06 (`53 §2`), `54 §2.4` |
| **Defect** | Documented platform behaviour delivers up to twice the daily budget on an individual day and caps monthly delivery at 30.4× the daily budget. The fixture reserves `30 × $6 = $180`, so one day of overdelivery makes the next boundary re-reservation fail and a healthy campaign pauses on day 2. Separately, `Standing(month)` used the 30-day rate period against a calendar-month window, understating by up to $6.00 in seven months of twelve. |
| **Affected artifacts** | `51 §3.2`, `51 §4`, `24 §3` K5, `26 §10.1`, `26 §2.6`, `35 §7` |
| **Required change** | `forward_exposure = rate × remaining_periods × (1 + overdelivery_allowance)` with the allowance declared per adapter from vendor documentation and cited. The month basis becomes the calendar window's actual day count, or the platform's own basis where larger. `STANDING_HEADROOM_SHORT` raised at the first *projected* shortfall. |
| **Applied change** | `26 §10.1` v1.2 replaces the multiplicative form with the **exposure-remainder form**, which is strictly better against the constructed attack and which `51 §4.3` demonstrates:<br>`standing_cap(s, w) = s.rate.amount × periods_basis(w, adapter)`<br>`forward_exposure(s, w, t) = max(0, standing_cap(s, w) − realised_spend(s, w, t))`<br>where `periods_basis(W_DAY) = daily_overdelivery_multiplier` and `periods_basis(W_MONTH) = max(days_in_window(w), monthly_basis_multiplier)`. Google Ads: `daily_overdelivery_multiplier = 2.0`, `monthly_basis_multiplier = 30.4`, both `DOCUMENTED` with a cited source. **Under this form the day-1 overdelivery case does not pause**: after $12.00 realised on day 1 of a 31-day January, `realised(12.00) + forward(174.00) = 186.00 ≤ 186.00`. The month basis is the calendar window's actual day count or the platform basis, whichever is larger: 28→30.4, 29→30.4, 30→30.4, 31→31. `24 §3` K5 raises `STANDING_HEADROOM_SHORT` when *projected* realised at window end exceeds `standing_cap`, evaluated on every re-reservation, not at the boundary. |
| **Invariant** | `I3` unchanged in form. `Standing(w)` now includes the allowance, so `MAL_total` rises and requires re-signature under `26 §10.3` — correct, because the allowance is authorised loss. |
| **Test** | `36 §2.8` VC-S4a/b: simulate 2× day-1 delivery and assert day-2 re-reservation succeeds and no pause occurs; simulate sustained delivery to `standing_cap` and assert pause with `STANDING_HEADROOM_SHORT` raised at first projection, before the boundary. `51 §4.3` recomputes 28/29/30/31-day months. |
| **Third review** | No |
| **Disposition** | **APPLIED WITH EXPLICIT RESIDUAL** |
| **Residual** | The 2.0 and 30.4 multipliers are `DOCUMENTED` for Google Ads and **not established for Meta**, whose published budget documentation does not state an equivalent bound. Meta is marked `UNMEASURED` / `IMPLEMENTATION_VALIDATION_REQUIRED` in `51 §3.2`, and until it is measured **no Meta rate grant is autonomy-eligible** — the conservative default. This does not affect the MVP, which declares one advertising adapter. |
| **Sections changed** | `26 §2.6`, `26 §10.1`, `24 §3` K5, `35 §7`, `51 §3.2`, `51 §4`, `36 §2` |

---

### SR-A1 — `I17` not evaluable by its owner

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Source attack** | AUD-01 (`53 §3`), `55 §3.1`, `55 §8` |
| **Defect** | `I17` is owned by Audit and requires enumerating the control journal; `24 §3` K11's declared inputs contain no control-journal read, and R10 removed the replica deliberately. The audit plane can detect mid-range omission and **cannot detect tail truncation**: a push stopping at seq 900 of 1,000 leaves a gap-free, hash-consistent audit side whose `row_count` anchor agrees with itself. |
| **Affected artifacts** | `24 §3` K11, `30 §5`, `30 §5.1`, `22 §3.1`, `36 §1`, `36 §13`, invariant registry |
| **Required change** | A periodic control-plane attestation carried as a journal row occupying its own sequence value, pushed on the same path, at a cadence strictly shorter than the anchor interval. Restate `I17` as transport completeness; move the unqualified completeness claim to `I8`. Add `I17e`. |
| **Applied change** | `30 §5.4` v1.2 is a new subsection specifying `JournalAttestation{company_id, max_journal_seq, row_count, head_hash, attested_at}` as `journal_row_kind = ATTESTATION`, allocated a real `journal_seq` from the same counter inside its own transaction, chained identically, pushed on the same replication path, at a declared cadence of **5 minutes** against an anchor interval of 60 minutes. `30 §5.5` v1.2 tabulates the six omission cases the mission requires — normal row omitted, attestation omitted, writer stopped entirely, last attestation stale, tail truncated, audit plane unavailable — each with its detector, its owner and its consequence. `30 §5`'s "Completeness" row is renamed **"Transport completeness"**. `I17`'s statement is restated as *gap-free and consistent with the latest received attestation*, and the unqualified claim is relocated to `I8` with its coverage limits attached at every citation. **No control-database read is introduced**, so R10's decision stands. |
| **Invariant** | New **I17e**: attestation rows are continuous at the declared cadence, and the latest received attestation's `max_journal_seq` and `row_count` are consistent with the audit store's holdings. `I17` restated. `I8` carries the unqualified claim and its limits. |
| **Test** | `36 §13.1` VC-A1a/b/c: truncate the push at seq 900 out of band and assert detection **from the audit plane alone with no control-database access**; suppress one attestation and assert it presents as a sequence gap; stop the writer entirely and assert `I17e` fires within `3 × cadence`. |
| **Third review** | **YES — Review Mechanism A** |
| **Disposition** | **APPLIED WITH EXPLICIT RESIDUAL** |
| **Residual** | Transport completeness does **not** prove that no effect was dispatched without a journal row on either side. That property belongs to `I8`'s inverse sweep from the audit plane's own vendor reads, whose coverage limits are stated at every citation. `55 §3.2` is correct that a control plane omitting from both sides produces perfect agreement, and this remediation does not close it — it stops claiming to. |
| **Sections changed** | `30 §5`, `30 §5.4` (new), `30 §5.5` (new), `24 §3` K11, `22 §3.1`, `36 §1`, `36 §13`, `35 §12.1`, `49 §3` |

---

### SR-A2 — degraded mode self-declared

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Source attack** | AUD-02 (`53 §3`), AUD-06 (`53 §3`), `55 §3.3`, `52 §1` Path B |
| **Defect** | The push, the `mirrored_at` column, the lag check and the degraded-mode flag are all control-plane state. From the audit side, provider outage and declining-to-push are the same observation, so the mode that relaxes the audit guarantee is entered on the word of the component it protects against — which also classifies each effect into the halt row governing it. |
| **Affected artifacts** | `30 §5.1`, `30 §3` V7, `24 §3` K10, `24 §3` K11, `25 §13`, `22 §3.1`, `35 §12.1` |
| **Required change** | Degraded mode entered only when the audit plane independently publishes `MIRROR_INPUT_STALL` from attestation continuity. Every effect dispatched under degradation tagged `DISPATCHED_UNMIRRORED`, and that tag set a mandatory input to `I8`'s next inverse sweep. `clock.source_record_ref` not null and RECORD-grade; never a model classification. |
| **Applied change** | `30 §5.6` v1.2 is a new subsection specifying a **three-state mirror state machine** — `NORMAL`, `UNCORROBORATED_STALL`, `CORROBORATED_DEGRADED` — with the critical inversion: **the uncorroborated state is stricter, not looser.** A unilateral control-plane declaration buys the control plane a *more* restrictive dispatch table (clock-bearing COMPENSABLE suspends rather than dispatches); only two-sided corroboration unlocks the relaxation. A compromised control plane declaring degradation therefore harms itself. `30 §5.7` v1.2 gives the full **actor/signal table** — who may create, attest, change and consume every degraded-mode signal — as the mission requires. `DISPATCHED_UNMIRRORED` is a not-null tag on every effect dispatched outside `NORMAL`, and `I8`'s next inverse sweep takes the tag set as a mandatory input. `24 §3` K10 v1.2 declares `clock.source_record_ref` not-null with an enumerated `kind` and a `source_record_contract` in `30 §9.1`; a clock is created by a **deterministic detector over a retained RECORD**, never by a triage classification. |
| **Invariant** | New **I17f** (two-sided): no effect carries `DISPATCHED_UNMIRRORED` without a concurrent audit-plane `MIRROR_INPUT_STALL` covering its dispatch time, **and** no audit-plane `MIRROR_INPUT_STALL` exists without a corresponding journaled control-plane declaration or an open `ATTESTATION_DIVERGENCE` incident. New **I56**: every statutory clock resolves to a RECORD-grade `source_record_ref`. |
| **Test** | `36 §13.2` VC-A2a/b/c: a control plane that stops pushing while the mirror is reachable must **fail** to enter `CORROBORATED_DEGRADED`; a triage worker classifying every case as a refund request must create **zero** live clocks; a genuine two-sided provider outage must be shown to reach the owner-override path rather than deadlocking silently. |
| **Third review** | **YES — Review Mechanism A, jointly with SR-A1** |
| **Disposition** | **APPLIED WITH EXPLICIT RESIDUAL** |
| **Residual** | If the audit plane is genuinely unavailable it cannot corroborate, so ACOS sits in `UNCORROBORATED_STALL` and clock-bearing compensable effects **suspend**, reintroducing the EM15/EM16 tension the split halt exists to manage. The escape is the owner override, now per recoverability class, time-boxed, count-capped, auto-expiring to halt, fully tagged, and requiring a distinct approver for a second override inside a declared interval (AUD-07's remediation, applied here because SR-A2 makes the override load-bearing). **A genuine two-sided outage costs an owner decision.** This is stated rather than resolved and is the third reviewer's question A3. |
| **Sections changed** | `30 §3` V7, `30 §5.1`, `30 §5.6` (new), `30 §5.7` (new), `30 §9.1` (new), `24 §3` K10, `24 §3` K11, `25 §13`, `22 §3.1`, `35 §12.1`, `36 §13` |

---

### SR-A3 — row canonicalisation undefined

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Source attack** | AUD-03 (`53 §3`), `55 §4` |
| **Defect** | The chain is over *"canonicalised rows"* and the canonicalisation is undefined. Postgres `jsonb` does not preserve key order, duplicate-key handling or numeric representation, so two independent triggers over one logical row can produce different bytes — continuous false `I41` criticals — and two logically different rows can canonicalise identically. |
| **Affected artifacts** | `30 §5`, `30 §5.1`, `50`, registry `I17d`, `I41` |
| **Required change** | Define the canonical byte string: fixed field order, RFC 8785-style canonicalisation for structured fields, fixed numeric scale per column, UTC timestamps at fixed precision, explicit null encoding. **Transmit** the canonical byte string and hash the transmitted bytes on both sides. The specification becomes a control artifact. |
| **Applied change** | `30 §5.3` v1.2 defines **`ACOS-JCS-1`** once, and only once, in the whole package: field order is the versioned journal-row column order; structured fields are RFC 8785 (JCS); numerics are emitted as decimal strings at a per-column declared scale with no exponent and no trailing-zero normalisation, so `25.0` and `25.00` hash **differently**; timestamps are RFC 3339 UTC with exactly six fractional digits; null is a single `0x00` sentinel distinct from the empty string; text is UTF-8 NFC; every field is length-prefixed with a four-byte big-endian length so concatenation is unambiguous. **The canonical byte string is what is transmitted**, and both sides hash the transmitted bytes. Re-serialising from `jsonb` on either side is prohibited and is a CI-checked code rule. `50 §2` gains **class 20 — journal canonicalisation specification** (owner, second factor; halt scope: all effects) because changing it reinterprets every historical hash. |
| **Invariant** | `I41` becomes achievable. `I17d` unchanged. |
| **Test** | `36 §13.3` VC-A3a/b: round-trip a row containing a structured field with keys inserted in two different orders and assert an identical `row_hash` on two instances; assert two rows differing only in numeric scale produce **different** hashes; assert a null and an empty string produce different hashes. `58 §12` item 3 records that the `jsonb` question is empirical and must be run across two real instances. |
| **Third review** | No |
| **Disposition** | **APPLIED** |
| **Sections changed** | `30 §5`, `30 §5.3` (new), `50 §2`, `36 §13`, `33 §6` |

---

### SR-A4 — gap-free sequencing mechanism unstated

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Source attack** | AUD-04 (`53 §3`), `55 §2` |
| **Defect** | Postgres sequences are non-transactional and gap on rollback, so they cannot deliver gap-freedom. The only correct implementations impose a company-scoped serialisation point on the effect path that no document states, and they introduce a lock-ordering requirement against the window balance row. |
| **Affected artifacts** | `30 §5.1`, `33` scale envelope, `25 §7`, registry `I17` |
| **Required change** | Specify `journal_counter(company_id, next_seq)` incremented inside the authorising transaction. Declare the lock order: window balance row, then journal counter. Record the per-company serialisation and its throughput consequence. State that `journal_seq` is not an input to the effect idempotency key. |
| **Applied change** | `30 §5.2` v1.2 specifies the counter row, the `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` acquisition, and the **declared lock order: `window_balance` rows first, in ascending `window_id` order, then `journal_counter`.** Ascending `window_id` is added because an effect reserves against *every* referenced window and two effects touching two windows in opposite orders deadlock independently of the counter. `33 §8` v1.2 records the per-company serialisation point, its DoS surface on the effect path, and the throughput envelope. `25 §7` v1.2 states explicitly that `journal_seq` is **not** an input to the idempotency key, so a serialisation-failure retry regenerates the same key. |
| **Invariant** | `I17` unchanged in content; its precondition is now specified and testable. |
| **Test** | `36 §13.4` VC-A4a/b/c: roll back an authorising transaction and assert no gap; run two concurrent authorisations for one company and assert adjacent sequence values; attempt reversed acquisition order across two code paths and assert no deadlock. `58 §12` item 1 retains this as an implementation-validation obligation. |
| **Third review** | No |
| **Disposition** | **APPLIED** |
| **Sections changed** | `30 §5.1`, `30 §5.2` (new), `33 §8`, `25 §7`, `36 §13`, `37 S1` |

---

### SR-A5 — re-push duplicates poison the chain and anchor

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Source attack** | AUD-05 (`53 §3`), `55 §2` |
| **Defect** | A crash between the audit acknowledgement and the `mirrored_at` update causes a re-push. Audit-side uniqueness is unspecified, so seq *N* is inserted twice, `I17`'s "exactly one audit row" fails, dispatch halts by class, and the `row_count` anchor is poisoned. **An ordinary crash produces a tamper signature.** |
| **Affected artifacts** | `30 §5.1`, registry `I17`, `I17b`, `I41` |
| **Required change** | `UNIQUE (company_id, journal_seq)` with `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`; the conflict path compares stored to re-pushed `row_hash`; same hash is a benign duplicate, different hash is tampering and a critical incident. `row_count` becomes `count(DISTINCT journal_seq)`. `mirrored_at` is advisory. |
| **Applied change** | `30 §5.2` v1.2 declares the constraint, the conflict path and the two outcomes with distinct incident types: `AUDIT_PUSH_DUPLICATE` (INFO, counted, no halt) and `AUDIT_SEQUENCE_COLLISION` (CRITICAL, halt by recoverability class, security path). `row_count` is `count(DISTINCT journal_seq)` everywhere it appears, including in `JournalAttestation` and `I17b`'s anchor tuple. `mirrored_at` is explicitly advisory and is **never** a correctness input to any invariant. |
| **Invariant** | `I17`, `I17b` become robust to retry. `I17`'s on-violation text distinguishes the two outcomes. |
| **Test** | `36 §13.5` VC-A5a/b: kill at the ack boundary, retry, and assert no incident and no halt; push a different row under an existing sequence value and assert `AUDIT_SEQUENCE_COLLISION` at CRITICAL. |
| **Third review** | No |
| **Disposition** | **APPLIED** |
| **Sections changed** | `30 §5.1`, `30 §5.2` (new), `24 §3` K11, `36 §13`, `35 §12.1` |

---

### SR-A6 — split-halt rows conflict

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Source attack** | AUD-10 (`53 §3`), `55 §6.2` |
| **Defect** | A $30 refund inside a live FTC clock, above the per-action approval floor, matches *"COMPENSABLE inside a live statutory clock — dispatch"* and *"anything above the per-action approval floor — halt"*. No precedence exists in `30 §5.1`, `24 §3` K11, `36 §6`, `22 §3.1` or `35 §12.1`, and `35 §12.1` walks exactly this case assuming the first row wins. **AUDA-01's signature reproduced inside its own remediation.** |
| **Affected artifacts** | `30 §5.1`, `24 §3` K11, `36 §6`, `22 §3.1`, `35 §12.1` |
| **Required change** | An ordered first-match list, plus the rule that an effect carrying a recorded approval is evaluated at row 3 or 5, not row 2. Rewrite `35 §12.1` to walk a floor-exceeding, clock-bearing refund. |
| **Applied change** | `30 §5.1` v1.2 item 4 is a single **ordered, first-match** precedence list with an explicit "exactly one row matches; evaluation stops at the first match" statement: (1) IRRECOVERABLE → halt; (2) above the approval floor **and not clock-bearing** → halt; (3) clock-bearing, any class except IRRECOVERABLE → dispatch, tagged `DISPATCHED_UNMIRRORED`; (4) COMPENSABLE discretionary → suspend; (5) REVERSIBLE → dispatch. **One authoritative precedence order exists and is stated once**; `24 §3` K11, `36 §6` and `22 §3.1` now cite `30 §5.1` rather than restating the table. The approval rule is stated: an effect carrying a recorded approval is evaluated at row 3 or row 5, never row 2, because the approval floor's purpose is satisfied. `35 §12.1` is rewritten to walk a **$30 floor-exceeding, clock-bearing** refund. Rows 3 and 5 apply only in `CORROBORATED_DEGRADED`; in `UNCORROBORATED_STALL` row 3 degrades to *suspend* per SR-A2. |
| **Invariant** | No new invariant; the table becomes deterministic. |
| **Test** | `36 §13.6` VC-A6: one fixture per row, **including one effect matching rows 2 and 3 simultaneously**, asserting the declared precedence; plus an approval-bearing effect asserting row 3. |
| **Third review** | No |
| **Disposition** | **APPLIED** |
| **Sections changed** | `30 §5.1`, `24 §3` K11, `36 §6`, `22 §3.1`, `35 §12.1` |

