### SR-L3 — order-driven cost excluded and undeclared

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Source attack** | LIM-03 (`53 §4`), `54 §4.3`, OOS-02 |
| **Defect** | Order-driven irrecoverable cost is excluded from `MAL_total` by `I30`, absent from **both** six-item exclusion lists, and its three governing controls have no declared values — in the class `33 §9` says dominates POD by volume. Order of magnitude from the fixture's own numbers: `200 × $35 = $7,000` against a displayed $600. |
| **Affected artifacts** | `51 §3.3`, `51 §6`, `26 §5`, `26 §10.4`, `30 §3` V2, registry `I30` |
| **Required change** | Declare the per-order fulfilment rate limit, the template whitelist and the fulfilment-to-settled-order ratio threshold with its evaluation window. Add a fifth displayed quantity, not folded in. Add order-driven cost as a seventh exclusion with its magnitude stated. State that the ratio's denominator is processor-settled and cite `I27`. |
| **Applied change** | `51 §3.4` v1.2 declares: **per-order fulfilment rate limit = 1**, a second fulfilment of the same order requiring TIER_2 approval; the **template whitelist** as a named control artifact (`50 §2` class 7) with the transactional order-state template set enumerated; the **fulfilment-to-settled-order ratio threshold = 1.05** over a trailing seven-calendar-day evaluation window on the DB clock, with `min_denominator = 20` below which the check returns `INSUFFICIENT_DATA`; and a **duplicate-fulfilment-attempt count > 0 per day** as a second anomaly trigger that does not depend on the denominator. The evaluation window is explicitly a **metric** window, not an exposure window, so `boundary_kind` does not apply to it. `OrderDriven_cost(w) = Σ order-driven fulfilments in w × unit_cost_p95(class)` is a **fifth displayed quantity** shown adjacent to `MAL_total` with its own utilisation and **not folded in** — it is COGS against revenue, not authorised loss. It is added to `26 §10.4` and `51 §6` as a **seventh exclusion** with its magnitude stated. `51 §3.4` states that the denominator is **processor-settled orders** and cites `I27`, so the dependency on R6 is explicit rather than inherited. |
| **Invariant** | `I30` unchanged. New **I57**: the fulfilment-to-settled-order ratio is below its declared threshold, computed with a processor-settled denominator. |
| **Test** | `36 §2.11` VC-L3a/b: attempt a second fulfilment of one order and assert refusal; fabricate orders through a compromised commerce adapter and assert the ratio detects them **because the denominator is settlement-derived**; assert `INSUFFICIENT_DATA` below `min_denominator` and that the per-order limit still holds there. |
| **Third review** | No |
| **Disposition** | **APPLIED WITH EXPLICIT RESIDUAL** |
| **Residual** | Two, both stated in `51 §3.4`. (a) **Below `min_denominator = 20` settled orders the ratio control is inert**, and the per-order limit is the only control. At Stage 2 volumes this is the ordinary condition, not the exception. (b) **`I57`'s integrity is inherited from `I27`**, which is R6 and out of this review's scope (OOS-02). If R6's processor-settlement corroboration is not implemented as specified, SR-L3 is a loss channel rather than a disclosure defect. Recorded as a named dependency, not resolved. `unit_cost` for order-driven fulfilment is `NOT_DECLARABLE_UNTIL_BUSINESS_MODEL_SELECTED` (Phase 1 left the model open); the displayed quantity computes from actual fulfilment cost records at runtime. |
| **Sections changed** | `26 §5`, `26 §10.1`, `26 §10.4`, `30 §3` V2, `51 §3.4`, `51 §4`, `51 §6`, `36 §2` |

